  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1059 AM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nHigh pressure continues to build and shifts east through Friday.\nWe remain on the dry side other than some high and mid level\nclouds streaming across the state. Temperatures will remain\nABOVE AVERAGE with 50s and 60s for highs. We will need to see if\nthe downslope pushes above the low/mid 60s across portions of\nthe Snake Plain, Magic Valley, and adjacent bench areas.  \n\nThe first wave or low crosses the state Friday evening through \nSaturday night. While we will see some light precipitation \nacross the Wood River Valley and Sawtooths later in the evening,\nthe bulk of the moisture falls after midnight through late \nSaturday afternoon and early evening. With the storm splitting \nand the main low drops southeast toward the Four Corners, the \nbulk of the moisture will be across the Wood River Valley, \nSawtooths and surrounding mountains, South Hills/Albion \nMountains, and the southeast highlands. Other areas will see \nlight precipitation overall. Those areas mentioned are looking \nat 0.25-0.50\" for totals in terms of rain or melted snow. There \na few spots across the highest elevations of the mountains \naround Ketchum and the South Hills/Albion Mountains are pushing \n0.50-0.70\". Amounts are similar across most versions of the GFS \nand ECMWF, including ensembles and AI versions...although there \nare some spatial differences with the amounts closer to the Utah\nborder. The AI version of the GFS is an extreme outlier is \npushing more than 1\" across the spine of the Sawtooths, which we\nare currently not going to account for due to the extremeness \nof the totals. Snowfall will generally be above 7000ft, although\nsome higher valleys like the Stanley Basin will see some light \naccumulations. Totals will generally up to 3\" with another 1-2\" \nwhere we see higher pockets of precipitation. Temperatures drop\nback into 30s and 40s for highs. We will see a few showers\nlingering Sunday, but mostly dry until the next low shifts\ninland. The Blend of Models brings moisture Sunday night, but\nthe overall trends have been to hold off until Monday...or at\nleast shifting the bulk of the moisture in Idaho. The pattern\nwith the storm looks very similar, with split flow and the bulk\nof the storm passing to our south. Temperatures briefly rebound\n5-10 degrees vs Saturday, but quickly drop again once the lows\nmoves our way.",
